---
const rows = [
  ["and", "Adds", "It was raining and we stayed inside."],
  ["so", "Result", "It was raining, so we stayed inside."],
  ["because", "Reason", "We stayed inside because it was raining."],
  ["but", "Contrast, coordinating", "It was raining, but we went outside."],
  ["although", "Contrast, subordinating", "Although it was raining, we went outside."]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Conjunctions</h1>
  <p class="topic-lead">Compare them</p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-sand">
    <h2>Same ideas, different relationship</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Word</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">It shows</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Example</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map(([word, job, example]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{word}</th>
              <td>{job}</td>
              <td>{example}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
  <p>
    <strong>So</strong> presents the result. <strong>Because</strong> presents the reason. Both can connect
    rain with staying inside, but they organise the sentence differently. Ask: what relationship does this
    conjunction show?
  </p>
  <div class="trap-card trap-compact">
    <section class="trap">
      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>And</strong> does not always join clauses. In “Tom and Sarah went shopping,” it joins two
        nouns.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>So</strong> answers “What happened as a result?” <strong>Because</strong> answers “Why?”
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>But</strong> is coordinating: “I was tired, but I continued.” <strong>Although</strong> is
        subordinating: “Although I was tired, I continued.” Both show contrast.
      </p>
      <p>
        “I haven’t finished <strong>yet</strong>” means up to now. “She was tired, <strong>yet</strong> she
        continued” is the conjunction.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>As</em> and <em>since</em> can show reason, but they can also show time: “as she walked,” “since
        Monday.” Read the whole sentence.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
